# Migrating `BackgroundProcessor` to .NET 8

## Key Problem

`Thread.Abort()` and `ThreadAbortException` are **removed** in .NET 8. Calling `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`. You must replace this with cooperative cancellation.

## Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Task _workerTask;
    private CancellationTokenSource _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerTask = Task.Run(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                // Pass token so blocking calls become cancellable
                var item = await GetNextItemAsync(token);
                await ProcessItemAsync(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Graceful shutdown requested
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                break;
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public async Task StopAsync(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        if (_cts == null || _workerTask == null)
            return;

        _cts.Cancel();

        // Wait for graceful completion with timeout
        var effectiveTimeout = timeout ?? TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5);
        if (await Task.WhenAny(_workerTask, Task.Delay(effectiveTimeout)) != _workerTask)
        {
            // Worker didn't stop in time — log a warning.
            // There is NO equivalent to Abort(); you must design
            // GetNextItemAsync/ProcessItemAsync to respect the token.
        }
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        _cts?.Cancel();
        _workerTask?.Wait(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2));
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

## Critical Pitfalls

| Issue | Details |
|-------|---------|
| **`Thread.Abort()` throws at runtime** | It compiles but throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 6+. |
| **`Thread.ResetAbort()` gone** | No equivalent exists — the entire abort mechanism is removed. |
| **Blocking calls can't be force-killed** | If `GetNextItem()` blocks on a socket/DB with no timeout, cancellation won't interrupt it. You **must** make blocking calls accept a `CancellationToken`. |
| **`Thread.Sleep` → `Task.Delay`** | Avoid blocking the thread pool; use `await Task.Delay(ms, token)`. |
| **No guaranteed hard stop** | .NET 8 has no safe way to forcibly terminate a thread. Design all operations to be cancellation-aware. |

## Migration Checklist

1. **Replace `Thread` with `Task.Run`** — uses the thread pool efficiently.
2. **Replace `volatile bool` with `CancellationToken`** — thread-safe, integrates with async APIs.
3. **Make blocking calls async + token-aware** — this is the hardest part. If wrapping legacy sync code, use `token.Register(() => resource.Close())` to unblock it.
4. **Replace `Thread.Abort()` with `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()`** — cooperative, not preemptive.
5. **Implement `IDisposable`** — clean up the `CancellationTokenSource`.

## If You Can't Make Blocking Calls Cancellable

As a last resort for truly uncooperative code:

```csharp
// Unblock a stuck synchronous call by closing its underlying resource
_cts.Token.Register(() =>
{
    _connection?.Close(); // forces the blocking Read() to throw
});
```

This is the only reliable way to interrupt non-cancellable blocking in .NET 8. There is **no** safe replacement for `Thread.Abort()`.
